'' 'procptr( <intrinsic> )' IS THE ADDRESS OF THE RUNTIME ENTRY POINT BEHIND IT.
'' Measured one spelling at a time, by reading what fbc assigns:
'' 'ZZ0$1 = (void*)&fb_Cls'. Sixty of them, and only the 'fb_' spelling was read
'' before -- warnings/rtl-prototypes.bas takes the address of every runtime entry the
'' compiler has and lost 224 statements to the two halves together.
''
'' TWO ARE NOT THE ENTRY POINT THE EXPRESSION FORM USES. 'pos( 0 )' is fb_Pos and a
'' bare 'pos' is fb_GetX; 'dir( pattern )' is fb_Dir and 'dir( )' is fb_DirNext.
'' procptr takes the no-argument one in both cases, which is not derivable from the
'' spelling. The radix family answers with the first overload -- fb_HEX_b, not
'' fb_HEX_l -- and 'allocate' with libc's malloc. 'stop' and 'system' are both fb_End,
'' the same entry the module epilogue calls, and 'pointcoord' is fb_GfxCursor.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/procptr-intrinsic.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_Cls
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_GetX
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_DirNext
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr malloc
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_HEX_b
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_End
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_Inkey
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr fb_GfxCursor
'' GOLDEN: expected/procptr-intrinsic.out
